{"reply":"In Chapter 7, Douglass writes: “It opened my eyes to the horrible pit, but to no ladder upon which to get out.” The sentence captures a painful distinction: understanding can make a problem more visible without yet providing a remedy or a means of acting. The passage presents that gap as burdensome, but it also suggests that seeing clearly is not the same as being helpless forever.

Your committee situation is very different, as you note, but the distinction may still be useful. You do not have to solve the whole repair system before taking a meaningful step. One possible “first rung” would be to create a shared record of missing requests—date submitted, issue, who received it, and current status—then bring a small, documented pattern to the committee with one concrete ask, such as a response deadline or a named person responsible for follow-up. That would turn a diffuse discovery into something the group can examine and act on.

